"""
Diesel engine driving an external inertia. RPM demand steps from 1500 to 1800 at t=2s;
the PI controller should bring the shaft to ~1800 RPM in a few seconds. Energy and fuel
integrators should accumulate accordingly. The damper load takes 355 kW at 1800 RPM,
below the 605 kW lower knot of the SFOC table, so `SFOC_valid` is 0 at the steady state.
"""
test component DieselEngineRamp
  engine = SimpleDieselEngine()
  inertia = RotationalComponents.Components.Inertia(J = 50)
  ground = RotationalComponents.Components.Damper(d = 10)   # acts as a load
  fixed = RotationalComponents.Components.Fixed()
  step = BlockComponents.Sources.Step(height = 300, start_time = 2.0, offset = 1500)
relations
  initial inertia.phi = 0
  initial inertia.w = 1500 * 2 * π / 60
  connect(step.y, engine.RPM_demand)
  connect(engine.flange, inertia.spline_a)
  connect(inertia.spline_b, ground.spline_a)
  connect(ground.spline_b, fixed.spline)
end

analysis SimpleDieselEngineTransient
  extends TransientAnalysis(stop = 30.0)
  model = DieselEngineRamp()
end

"""
Shaft speed imposed on the engine by a velocity source: 1500 RPM at t=0, through zero
at t=10 s, -500 RPM at t=15 s and back to 1500 RPM at t=30 s, with a constant 1500 RPM
demand. While the shaft is dragged backwards the PI saturates at the low-speed torque
limit, so the engine resists with positive torque and `ShaftPower` is negative; the fuel
rate must stay at zero (no idle fuel here) and the cumulative fuel must not decrease.

The analysis declares the two zero-power crossings as `tstops = [10, 20]`, so the solver
ends a step at each and the dense fuel counter is nondecreasing. Without them one step
spans the crossing and the interpolated counter (dense output or `saveat`) falls back by
1.5e-5 kg.
"""
test component DieselEngineReversing
  engine = SimpleDieselEngine()
  drive = RotationalComponents.Sources.VelocitySource(ref_type = RotationalComponents.Sources.ReferenceType.Exact())
  fixed = RotationalComponents.Components.Fixed()
  demand = BlockComponents.Sources.Constant(k = 1500)
relations
  connect(demand.y, engine.RPM_demand)
  connect(engine.flange, drive.spline)
  connect(drive.support, fixed.spline)
  drive.w_ref = (2 * π / 60) * (500 + 1000 * cos(2 * π * time / 30))
end

analysis SimpleDieselEngineReversingTransient
  extends TransientAnalysis(stop = 30.0, tstops = [10.0, 20.0])
  model = DieselEngineReversing()
end

"""
Engine with zero RPM demand on a shaft at rest, with an explicit no-load fuel rate. The
value 0.002 kg/s is a synthetic test number, not a calibration or a manufacturer figure. Nothing moves, the shaft
work stays zero, and the fuel counter grows linearly at exactly `m_dot_idle`.
"""
test component DieselEngineIdle
  engine = SimpleDieselEngine(m_dot_idle = 0.002)
  inertia = RotationalComponents.Components.Inertia(J = 50)
  ground = RotationalComponents.Components.Damper(d = 10)
  fixed = RotationalComponents.Components.Fixed()
  demand = BlockComponents.Sources.Constant(k = 0)
relations
  initial inertia.phi = 0
  initial inertia.w = 0
  connect(demand.y, engine.RPM_demand)
  connect(engine.flange, inertia.spline_a)
  connect(inertia.spline_b, ground.spline_a)
  connect(ground.spline_b, fixed.spline)
end

analysis SimpleDieselEngineIdleTransient
  extends TransientAnalysis(stop = 100.0)
  model = DieselEngineIdle()
end

"""
Engine held inside its SFOC table for a whole run. Constant 1800 RPM demand on a shaft
that starts at 1500 RPM; the damper takes 799 kW at 1800 RPM. The torque limit is reached
at once, so the brake power starts at 864 kW and stays between 639 and 1121 kW, inside
the 605–1210 kW table: `SFOC_valid` is 1 throughout and `Fuel_extrapolated` stays at
exactly zero. The rate has no kink and the coverage flag no jump in this run, so no
`tstops` are needed; sample the counters at the accepted steps.

The load is synthetic and the SFOC table is the default upstream reference table, not an
OEM calibration; `m_dot_idle` is zero.
"""
test component DieselEngineInRange
  engine = SimpleDieselEngine()
  inertia = RotationalComponents.Components.Inertia(J = 50)
  ground = RotationalComponents.Components.Damper(d = 22.5)   # acts as a load
  fixed = RotationalComponents.Components.Fixed()
  demand = BlockComponents.Sources.Constant(k = 1800)
relations
  initial inertia.phi = 0
  initial inertia.w = 1500 * 2 * π / 60
  connect(demand.y, engine.RPM_demand)
  connect(engine.flange, inertia.spline_a)
  connect(inertia.spline_b, ground.spline_a)
  connect(ground.spline_b, fixed.spline)
end

analysis SimpleDieselEngineInRangeTransient
  extends TransientAnalysis(stop = 30.0)
  model = DieselEngineInRange()
end
